commit e89813dcce7c623e540ffdbedfbe42aeb0e69aa8
Author: rbops-bot <rbops-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Mon Oct 12 09:59:41 2026 +0000

    phase-050 round 4: prove a module refusal is catchable, drop the stray artifact
    
    The phase asks for "each a caught RuntimeError naming the function, never a
    panic". The inherited test proved the refusal -- non-zero exit, the function
    named in stderr, no "panicked" -- which is the process-level half. Nothing
    proved the other half: that a program can catch the refusal and carry on from
    it, so a builtin that unwound instead of raising would have passed.
    
    edge_a_bad_module_call_is_a_catchable_error_and_not_an_abort runs three of the
    same bad calls inside try ... catch error and asserts stdout is exactly
    "caught\nafter\n" -- "after" is there only if the statement past the try ran.
    It also pins the boundary that makes those three mean something: an
    AnalyzerError is not a RuntimeError, so nosuchmodule.read("x") inside the same
    try leaves the program dead, and the test says so.
    
    Also deletes `nope`, a one-byte file an earlier round's bytes.write program left
    at the repository root by naming a path relative to the working directory. A
    full cargo test --all-targets now leaves git status clean.
    
    Gates: cargo fmt --check pass; cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings pass;
    cargo test --all-targets 1427 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored across 53 result
    lines; examples/ and modules/ 9 files, 0 failures. rbops/verify.sh is not in
    this checkout and was not run -- REPORT.md claims nothing about it.

