22:12. [Standard Library](#standard-library)
23-13. [Grammar](#grammar)
24-
25----
26-
27-## Introduction
28-
29-Redblue is a general-purpose programming language designed for readability. Its syntax is based on natural English sentences, making code self-documenting and accessible to beginners while remaining powerful enough for professional software development.
30-
31-### Design Goals
32-
33-1. **Readability**: Code should be understandable by reading it aloud
34-2. **Simplicity**: Minimal keywords, no symbolic clutter
35-3. **Power**: Full support for modern programming paradigms
36-4. **Performance**: Comparable to Python for typical workloads
37-
38-### Hello World
39-
40-```redblue
41-say "Hello, World!"
42-```
43-
44----
45-
46-## Lexical Structure
47-
48-### Identifiers
49-
50-Identifiers name variables, functions, and objects.
51-
52-```
53-identifier = letter { letter | digit | '_' }
54-letter = 'a'..'z' | 'A'..'Z' | unicode_letter
55-```
56-
57-**Valid identifiers:**
58-```redblue
59-set name to "Alice"
60-set _private to 10
61-set camelCase to 20
62-set 名前 to "Japanese"  // Unicode allowed
63-```
64-
65-### Keywords
66-
67-Redblue reserves 47 words, each with a rule in the parser that reads it:
68-
69-| Category | Keywords |
70-|----------|----------|
71-| Variables | set, constant, to, is, nothing, yes, no |
72-| Control | if, then, else, end, unless |
73-| Loops | for, each, in, from, times, while, repeat, until |
74-| Jumps | break, skip, return, give back |
75-| Errors | might fail, try, catch, finally |
76-| Output | say, print |
77-| Modules | module, import, export, as |
78-| Logic | and, or, not, mod |
79-| Tests | test, expect |
80-| Objects | object, has, can, this, extends |
81-
82-`give back` and `might fail` are two words each, which is why 47 words make 45
83-keyword forms. Every other word here is reserved on its own. A word that is not
84-on this list is an ordinary identifier; see the appendix at the end for the ones
85-that used to be listed and are not any more.
86-
87-### Literals
88-
89-```redblue
90-// Numbers
91-set n to 42
92-set f to 3.14
93-set neg to -10
94-
95-// Text (Strings)
96-set s to "Hello, World!"
97-set multi to "Line 1
98-Line 2"
99-
100-// Boolean
101-set flag to yes
102-set flag to no
103-
104-// Nothing
105-set empty to nothing
106-
107-// Lists
108-set arr to [1, 2, 3]
109-set words to ["hello", "world"]
110-
111-// Records
112-set person to record {
113-    name: "Alice",
114-    age: 30
115-}
116-```
117-
118-### Comments
119-
120-```redblue
121-// Single line comment
122-
123-// This is a multi-line
124-// comment spanning
125-// multiple lines
126-```
127-
128-### Whitespace
129-
130-Whitespace is ignored except as a statement separator. Use newlines or `end` to close blocks.
131-
132----
133-
134-## Types
135-
136-### Primitive Types
137-
138-| Type | Description | Example |
139-|------|-------------|---------|
140-| `number` | Integers and decimals | `42`, `3.14`, `-10` |
141-| `text` | Unicode strings | `"Hello"` |
142-| `yes/no` | Boolean values | `yes`, `no` |
143-| `nothing` | Null/void | `nothing` |
144-
145-### Complex Types
146-
147-```redblue
148-// List of a type
149-set numbers to list of number
150-set names to list of text
151-
152-// Record type
153-set Point to record { x: number, y: number }
154-
155-// Object type
156-set Person to object
157-```
158-
159-### Type Inference
160-
161-Redblue infers types automatically:
162-
163-```redblue
164-set x to 10        // x is number
165-set name to "Hi"   // name is text
166-```
167-
168-### Explicit Types
169-
170-```redblue
171-set x to 10 as number
172-set name to "Hi" as text
173-```
174-
175----
176-
177-## Variables and Values
178-
179-### Declaration
180-
181-```redblue
182-set name to value
183-set x to 1
184-set greeting to "Hello"
185-```
186-
187-### Assignment
188-
189-```redblue
190-set name to new_value
191-```
192-
193-### Constants
194-
195-```redblue
196-constant PI to 3.14159
197-constant MAX_SIZE to 1000
198-```
199-
200----
201-
202-## Expressions
203-
204-### Arithmetic
205-
206-```redblue
207-set sum to 1 + 2
208-set diff to 5 - 3
209-set product to 4 * 3
210-set quotient to 10 / 2
211-set remainder to 10 mod 3
212-```
213-
214-#### Numeric semantics
215-
216-A `number` is a 64-bit float. Every operation on numbers is **total**: it either
217-returns a number or fails with a runtime error. There is no third outcome in
218-which an operation returns something that is not a number.
219-
220-| Expression | Outcome |
221-|---|---|
