All four findings are fixed, each proven to fail on the defect it names, and all four gates are green.

**Finding 1 (BLOCKER)** — `src/stdlib.rs:719` `module_member_name` unwrapped `module_member` → `member` for *any* module × *any* builtin, so `console.input`, `math.random`, `json.uppercase`, `csv.type_of` all answered. It now unwraps only for a pair `MODULE_MEMBERS` holds (new `stdlib::module_has_member`, `src/stdlib.rs:115`) **and** whose bare name is registered. Everything else falls through to the existing `Module '<m>' has no function '<x>'` refusal in both engines.

**Finding 2 (MAJOR)** — `length` was reachable as `text.length` and `list.length` (two existing tests pin it) but was in no table. Both are now named in `MODULE_MEMBERS`, so the table *is* the surface the resolver enforces, not only the one the gate walks.

**Finding 3 (MAJOR)** — `dotted_calls` lowercased both halves while `is_module` is exact-match, so a document writing `JSON.Parse` reconciled clean and then died as `Unknown variable`. Dotted pairs are now kept as written; headings fold onto the module spelling case-insensitively (`module_spelling`), because a heading is prose and README capitalises `### Files` / `### JSON and CSV`.

**Finding 4 (MINOR)** — the member-table check now compares sets (both sides sorted).

Two new gates: `edge_a_flat_builtin_is_not_spelled_as_a_member_of_any_other_module` (every unlisted `module.builtin` pair refused, in **both** engines; reserved words like `bytes.expect` are asserted as `ParserError`, not skipped) and `edge_a_module_does_not_answer_a_function_the_documents_say_it_does_not_have` (the three SPEC promises by name, with flat `input`/`random` still asserted to answer). One existing test was *strengthened*, not weakened: `text_split_join_test.rs`'s reachability table was building `json.json_parse(..)` — a spelling that only worked because of the blocker — so it now names its dotted member separately and calls `json.parse(..)`.

